\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The classical Abelian action at small spacing}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For #{d\geq2}, fix a flat #{d}-torus and smooth periodic real fields #{A_j}, with their derivative bounds fixed as #{a\to0} along torus-fitting meshes. For unit-charge midpoint links
##{U_j(x)=\exp\bigl(iaA_j(ax+\tfrac a2e_j)\bigr),\qquad
F_{jk}=\partial_jA_k-\partial_kA_j,}}

\p{\ref{fdip-007F} gives
##{S_g(a)=\frac{\beta a^{4-d}}2\int\sum_{j<k}F_{jk}^2\,\extd x+O(\beta a^{5-d}).}}

\p{With #{g>0} and #{\beta=a^{d-4}/g^2},
##{S_g(a)=\frac1{2g^2}\int\sum_{j<k}F_{jk}^2\,\extd x+O(a)
=\frac1{4g^2}\int\sum_{j,k}F_{jk}^2\,\extd x+O(a).}}

\p{The two sums differ because the latter counts both orders. The fermion sees #{U_j^q=\ue{\ui qaA_j}}; that charge does not change the declared unit-charge plaquette normalization. If a coupling is absorbed into #{A}, it must also be kept in this comparison. For #{d=1}, the canonical plaquette action and both curvature sums are empty and equal to zero. This is evaluation of a classical action on fixed smooth backgrounds, with the stated remainder. It supplies no limit of the connection probability laws, quantum continuum photons or continuum QED.}
